# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the GPU and compiler tests (the full-verification TSan
# job). lavapipe, Mesa's CPU Vulkan driver, isn't built with TSan, so the
# locking between its own worker threads reports as races (seen with Mesa
# 25.2.8-0ubuntu0.24.04.4: a mutex locked on one driver thread while another
# destroys it, both stacks inside the driver). The validation layer has the
# same problem, and is turned off in that job.
#
# A race with a frame in the driver on either side is suppressed. Our own
# threads (the render thread, the logger, the queues) race without it, and are
# still reported.
race:libvulkan_lvp.so

# lavapipe compiles shaders with LLVM on its rasterizer threads (llvmpipe-0,
# -1, ...), and LLVM isn't TSan-built either: with multisampling (milestone 5,
# D-027) its threads race inside libLLVM alone, with no lavapipe frame on
# either side (seen with LLVM 20.1, Ubuntu 24.04: StringMapImpl and
# IEEEFloat). Suppressed likewise.
race:libLLVM.so

# GDAL (the terrain compiler's tests), likewise not TSan-built: its own
# mutexes, taken in different orders on different threads, report as
# potential deadlocks with both locks inside libgdal (seen with GDAL 3.8.4,
# Ubuntu 24.04). Each worker opens its own datasets, which GDAL supports
# from several threads. Races are not suppressed.
deadlock:libgdal.so
